`static`, each .cu file that includes sumcheck.cuh gets its own copy. The stacked reduction uses a different approach with atomic adds instead, so this kernel might not even be used anywhere.\n\nI need to create new extern \"C\" launcher functions in zerocheck_round0.cu and logup_round0.cu that call this batched reduction kernel. Actually, a better approach would be to write a single batched launcher function that combines both the evaluation kernel and the batched reduction in one call, similar to how the existing non-batched launcher works.\n\nWriting batched launcher function... I need to handle two runtime variants for the GLOBAL template parameter, and I'm calculating the memory requirements for the pre-zeroed temporary buffer that holds the batched sums. For typical configurations with 500 AIRs, this comes to around 768 KB for standard fields or 1.5 MB for logup with extended field elements.\n\nNow I'm checking whether the performance target is realistic—the current GPU time is about 464ms, and with batching I'd be distributing work across all 108 SMs instead of using just 3-12 per kernel, which should give meaningful speedup even accounting for the ~5,600 total blocks across all AIRs. each can run multiple blocks (typically 2-8 depending on register/shmem usage)\nSo ~108 * 4 = 432 blocks can run concurrently\n\n5,610 / 432 ≈ 13 \"waves\" of blocks\nTime per block ≈ 464ms / (935 kernels * 1 wave each) = ~0.5ms per wave\n13 waves * 0.5ms ≈ 6.5ms!\n\nWait, that seems too optimistic. Let me reconsider.\n\nActually, the current time is 464ms for ~935 sequential kernels. Each kernel takes average 0.5ms. With batching, all kernels run in parallel.\n\nThe total work (thread-cycles) is the same. If we have 108 SMs and each block takes the same amount of time:\n- Total block time = 5,610 blocks * 0.5ms/block... no, that's not right.\n\nLet me think about this differently. The current execution is 935 kernels running sequentially, each taking ~0.5ms on average, which adds up to the measured 464ms. Each kernel only uses 3-12 SMs, leaving most idle. With batching into a single kernel of 5,610 blocks, I need to figure out how long each block actually takes. Since each original kernel had ~6 blocks that ran in parallel and completed in 0.5ms, a single block's execution time is that same 0.5ms divided by however many blocks can overlap.\n\nWith 108 SMs available and assuming each SM can run 2-4 blocks concurrently based on resource constraints, I could fit around 216-432 blocks running simultaneously. That means 5,610 blocks would need roughly 13-26 waves to complete, giving a total time of 6.5-13ms. But this assumes perfect scaling and uniform work distribution, which seems optimistic given that different kernels have varying complexity.\n\nLooking at the actual numbers, the per-kernel execution times range from 275 to 493 microseconds, and there's also about 9-19ms of launch overhead spread across 935 kernel calls. The target improvement is 2-3x (reducing 464ms to 250-350ms), not the 30x+ that pure SM math would suggest. This gap likely points to memory bandwidth being the real constraint rather than compute, or cache contention when batching kernels that access different memory regions, or register pressure reducing occupancy when I try to pack more work together.\n\nThe 464ms of coset_parallel time is the only improvable portion—the 96ms from large AIRs and 10ms of CPU work won't change. Even a 3x speedup on the GPU work gets me to around 261ms total, which fits the target range. The task's estimate is realistic, so I should stop second-guessing the math and move forward with the implementation plan.\n\nNow I need to check whether zerocheck and logup can be batched in the same kernel or if they need separate launches due to their different signatures and logic.\n\nThey'll need separate batched launches, but since they're currently back-to-back on the same stream anyway, batching them separately (all zerocheck kernels together, then