iled rules with their lengths, but there's no shared data across AIRs—logup doesn't even use lambda powers, so that parameter doesn't apply here. The `d_intermediates` pointer is only needed when `GLOBAL=true`, since the `GLOBAL=false` case uses local registers instead. The kernel can safely ignore it when that template parameter is false, but for the global case, each AIR needs its own pre-allocated intermediates buffer sized according to `buffer_size`, `num_x`, `num_cosets`, and `max_temp_bytes`.\n\nFor the batched kernel, I'd compute the intermediates size per-AIR on the Rust side using the launch parameters and buffer threshold logic...\n\nDetermining buffer sizes per AIR... I'm realizing there's a circular dependency here—the grid size depends on the intermediates buffer size, but the buffer size calculation needs the grid size. However, looking at the actual launch parameter code, it computes the initial grid and block dimensions first, then adjusts grid.x downward based on the memory constraints, so I can use that adjusted grid.x value to determine the final intermediates allocation size.\n\nThe batched kernel uses a different launch configuration with a higher max_x_blocks limit, which means each AIR could allocate larger intermediates than in the single-AIR case. This creates a potential memory issue when batching multiple GLOBAL=true AIRs together, since the per-AIR budget was designed for one AIR at a time. That said, this is probably manageable in practice since most AIRs use GLOBAL=false and the few that need GLOBAL=true can either run individually or be batched carefully in small groups.\n\nThe simplest approach would be to batch only GLOBAL=false AIRs and keep GLOBAL=true AIRs on the individual launch path, which sidesteps the memory concern entirely while still capturing most of the batching benefits. But I'm realizing there's a constraint I need to work around: the kernel allocates a fixed-size local buffer when GLOBAL=false, and if an AIR's buffer_size exceeds that threshold, indexing into the local buffer would go out of bounds.\n\nSo the hard constraint is that AIRs with buffer_size greater than the compile-time threshold must use GLOBAL=true. That means my final approach is to batch only the GLOBAL=false coset-parallel AIRs while keeping GLOBAL=true ones on individual launches. At APC 300 with 935 coset-parallel kernels, most should fall into the GLOBAL=false category, so even if a few dozen require GLOBAL=true, the overall speedup should still be significant.\n\nLet me work through the math: if roughly 90% are GLOBAL=false, that's about 840 batched kernels versus 95 individual ones. The individual launches would take around 47.5ms total, and the batched kernel another 15-50ms, bringing the whole coset-parallel portion down to 62-97ms from the original 464ms—a solid 5-7x improvement.\n\nThe key is to measure how many AIRs actually fall into each category during implementation, since that determines whether we get most of the benefit or need to tackle GLOBAL=true batching as a follow-up. I should focus the plan on the simpler GLOBAL=false batching first, which captures the majority of the gains.\n\nFor the actual plan, I'll design batching around template parameters for GLOBAL=false with runtime NEEDS_SHMEM, let individual GLOBAL=true AIRs continue launching separately, include a diagnostic to measure the split, and note that GLOBAL=true batching can be added later if the data shows it's necessary. One more consideration: consolidating the zerocheck and logup batched kernels into a single kernel might be cleaner than maintaining two separate ones.\n\nActually, that won't work—zerocheck and logup have completely different kernel logic with different accumulation patterns and output types, so keeping them separate is the right call. The total batched kernel launches come down to just 2 (one zerocheck, one logup) plus 2 batched reductions, giving us 4 launches for all batched AIRs instead of around 1870. Individual launches for GLOBAL=true and non-coset-parallel AIRs remain fast since there aren't many of them and they're already well-utilized.\n\nNow I need to figure out the blockDim.x configuration for the batched kernel...\n\nDetermining block dimensions based on kernel parameters...\n\nI see the issue now—for small AIRs like num_x = 1 with skip_domain = 4, the block size becomes just 4 threads, which is inefficient. The solution is to use a fixed block size of 128 threads across all AIRs in the batched kernel, letting smaller AIRs have idle threads that simply don't participate in iterations.\n\nHowever, the in-block reduction logic assumes all threads contribute to the sum, so threads that process zero x_int values will still have sum = 0 and participate in the reduction correctly.\n\nFor the batched kernel, I need to set block.x = 128 across all AIRs, which works fine since idle threads just contribute zero. The grid.x dimension should be the maximum x_blocks count across all AIRs, where each AIR's x_blocks is calculated as ceil(skip_domain * num_x / block.x). With block.x = 128 and skip_domain = 4, this means an AIR with num_x = 1 needs 1 block, while num_x = 100 needs ceil(400 / 128) blocks.\n\nGiven the coset-parallel threshold of num_x * skip_domain < 32768, the maximum num_x for skip_domain = 4 is 8191, which gives ceil(32764 / 128) = 256 max x_blocks. A 3D grid of 256 * 4 * 500 blocks would be 512,000 total, but most would be idle since not every AIR has that many x_blocks—this is wasteful with high variance in AIR sizes. I should explore a 1D or 2D grid approach where each block handles a specific AIR instead.\n\nAlternatively, I could group AIRs by similar sizes and batch each group separately to reduce waste, but that adds complexity. For now, I'll stick with the simpler 3D grid approach and accept some inefficiency—the idle blocks would just return immediately in about a microsecond each, which totals around 504 milliseconds across 500 AIRs. We can optimize this later if it becomes a bottleneck.\n\nActually, that's worse than what we're doing now. The 3D grid approach breaks down when there's high variance in how many x_blocks each AIR has. Let me try a different strategy: flatten the (AIR, x_block) pairs into a 2D grid where one dimension is the total x_blocks across all AIRs and the other is the max number of cosets. I'd use a cumulative offset array to map each block index back to its AIR, then binary search within the kernel to find which AIR a given block belongs to—though binary search in a kernel might not be ideal.\n\nA cleaner option is precomputing two small mapping arrays: one that stores which AIR each block belongs to, and another that stores the block's local index within that AIR. With around 1,870 total blocks, these arrays are tiny and easily fit in memory. Or I could embed this mapping directly in the metadata by having one entry per block instead of per AIR, but that inflates the metadata size.\n\nActually, the simplest approach is just to use blockIdx.x directly for the (air, x_block) pair with flat metadata—the grid dimensions become the total work items across all AIRs by the max number of cosets.\n\nLooking at how the stacked reduction handles this: each block loads its own metadata entry indexed by blockIdx.x, so there's one metadata entry per block. For Round 0 batching, I'd need one metadata entry per (AIR, x_block) pair, which is a lot of entries. The coset dimension maps to grid.y, so each (blockIdx.x, blockIdx.y) combination represents a unique work unit.\n\nThe issue is that storing metadata per blockIdx.x duplicates the same AIR metadata across multiple x_blocks. To avoid this duplication while keeping indexing simple, I'll use a two-level approach: an array mapping each blockIdx.x to its AIR index, and a separate per-AIR metadata array. This adds one indirection but eliminates redundancy.\n\nWriting the kernel implementation... Writing the kernel logic... I need to precompute the `air_for_block` and `block_offset_in_air` arrays on the Rust side—about 1,870 entries total, which is only ~15 KB. I could compute `block_offset_in_air` in the kernel from `blockIdx.x` and segment offsets to avoid the extra upload, but precomputing it is cleaner and sidesteps divergent binary search. The kernel needs `local_block_x` for computing thread indices anyway, so I'll keep both arrays precomputed.\n\nIn the batched kernel, the stride calculation needs to use per-AIR block counts from `meta.num_x_blocks` instead of `gridDim.x`, since each AIR has its own grid dimensions. I'm verifying that the batched reduction correctly handles multiple AIRs by summing blocks within their respective ranges—AIR 0 gets blocks 0-2 and AIR 1 gets blocks 3-4. For the intermediates buffer when GLOBAL is false, the kernel allocates a small local array and uses it as a register-based buffer instead of allocating global memory.\n\nThe identity coset optimization carries over to the batched kernel since blockIdx.y still represents the coset index, allowing the skip_ntt flag to work the same way.\n\nNow I'm ready to write out the implementation plan with clear sections covering the current code path, the bottleneck, and the specific changes needed for batching.\n\nActually, before I finalize this, let me quickly estimate the temporary buffer size for the batched kernel to make sure it's feasible. For zerocheck, I'm looking at roughly 539 coset-parallel instances with an average of 1-2 blocks each, so around 800 total blocks. The buffer size depends on the maximum number of cosets times the skip domain size, which I need to verify won't exceed reasonable GPU memory constraints.\n\nWith a constraint degree of 3, max cosets of 2, and skip domain of 4, that gives me a depth of 8, so the zerocheck buffer comes to about 100 KB—totally manageable. For logup, I'm working with around 600 blocks, and with a constraint degree of 3, max cosets of 3, and skip domain of 4, the depth is 12, which puts the buffer at roughly 225 KB for the extended field representation. Both are well within acceptable limits, so the batching approach should work fine.\n\nThe output buffers themselves are also quite small—zerocheck needs about 69 KB and logup needs 152 KB. Combined with the metadata and mapping arrays, which total around 200 KB across the air metadata, block mappings, and segment offsets, I'm looking at a very reasonable memory footprint overall. There's no concern here about memory constraints.\n\nNow I'm wondering whether I actually need both the `air_for_block` and the segment offset arrays, or if one could serve the purpose of the other.\n\nSince `segment_offsets` gives me cumulative block counts per AIR, I can use binary search to find which AIR a given block belongs to—for blockIdx.x = 4, I'd search and find it maps to AIR 1 with a local offset of 1. The binary search itself is fast in a kernel, taking about log2(num_airs) steps, and since all threads in a block share the same blockIdx.x, they all perform the same search with no warp divergence. This means I could eliminate the `air_for_block` array entirely and just rely on `segment_offsets` for the lookup.\n\nBut precomputing `air_for_block` is simpler and the memory cost is negligible—just a few KB—so I'll stick with that approach to keep the kernel straightforward. I can even optimize further by using only `air_for_block` and `segment_offsets` together: look up the AIR index from the precomputed array, then subtract the corresponding segment offset to get the local block coordinate.\n\nNow I'm checking whether all coset-parallel AIRs use the same thread block dimensions, since the launch configuration varies based on `skip_domain` and `num_x`.\n\nLooking at the math: with a max of 128 threads per block and skip_domain of 4, smaller AIRs get proportionally smaller blocks (4, 8 threads) while larger ones max out at 128. For the batched kernel, I'd need a uniform block size—using 128 across the board means some AIRs will have idle threads in their reductions, but that's acceptable since most AIRs already hit 128 anyway, and the wasted resources for tiny AIRs is negligible compared to the complexity of variable block sizes.\n\nNow I'm thinking through the reduction logic itself—the kernel code that accumulates partial sums across threads in shared memory, handling the lane-based summation to combine results from different thread groups.\n\nActually, I'm realizing there's a subtle issue here: threads that don't execute any iterations of the inner loop might skip writing to shared memory entirely. But looking at the current single-AIR kernel, threads without work still initialize their sum to zero and participate in the synchronization, so the reduction still works correctly. In the batched version, early returns only happen at the block level when the coset index is invalid, which means entire blocks are skipped rather than individual threads within a block.\n\nNow I'm looking at how block dimensions are determined based on temporary memory constraints. The code calculates how many x values each thread should process, then adjusts the grid and block dimensions accordingly to stay within memory limits. If the number of x values per thread fits within the grid, it reduces the grid size; otherwise it keeps the grid at 1 and increases the block size instead.\n\nFor the batched kernel with GLOBAL=false, the intermediate capacity is zero, so no adjustment occurs and block.x remains at its default of 128. For the non-batched case with GLOBAL=true, the existing logic handles memory constraints as needed.\n\nWith the batched setup, block.x ends up being 128 (limited by MAX_THREADS), and now I need to figure out how many x_blocks each AIR requires based on dividing the total x values by the block size.\n\nLet me trace through the Rust code to see how `num_x` and `height` are currently computed for each AIR, since that determines the block count calculation. I'm seeing how `n_lift` is computed from `n_per_trace` and clamped to zero, which determines `num_x` as a power of two. The actual trace height is separate, and `num_x` can differ from height when traces are lifted. For the batched kernel, I need to calculate `num_x_blocks` by dividing the skip domain times `num_x` by the block size, and then check whether this product stays below the coset-parallel threshold of 32768 to decide on the execution strategy.\n\nThe GLOBAL=false constraint adds a buffer size limit of 16 that affects batching decisions but not the block count itself. Now I'm looking at the shared memory allocation in the CUDA kernel—specifically how much space is needed for the sum reduction and NTT buffers with a block size of 128. Reviewing the kernel signature and grid layout...\n\nThe kernel uses a 2D grid where each block handles one segment and one output dimension, reading the corresponding block sums and accumulating them into the final output.\n\nSetting up the launch with a grid of `(num_segments, d)` dimensions—around 500 by 12 in this case—and using block-level reduction with shared memory to efficiently sum the values. I need to be careful about the memory layout here. When I reinterpret the tmp_sums_buffer from FracExt to FpExt units, the stride doubles since each FracExt contains two FpExt components, so the `d` parameter and grid dimensions need to account for that doubling. I need to check whether `batched_final_reduce_block_sums` is actually being used anywhere in the codebase, since it's defined as a static kernel but might not be called. Looking at the existing reduction code, it seems to rely on atomic operations instead of this batched approach, so this function could be completely unused. Let me verify the implementation is correct by examining the kernel logic and making sure it compiles and functions properly when needed. I see the issue now—the batched version actually does declare `shared` from `extern __shared__` just like the non-batched version, so that's not the problem. Let me look more carefully at what's different between them. Writing the kernel function...\n\nThe block-level reduction happens next, then the result gets written to output if we're the first thread. I was concerned about the shared memory declaration, but it's properly initialized from extern, so that's fine. Since this function is static in the header, both files that include it will get their own copy, which works correctly. The main thing to verify is that it actually compiles when called, but that should be straightforward to check during implementation.\n\nNow I'm ready to document the key design decisions. I'll focus on the 2D grid approach with a flat mapping array to avoid wasting space on a 3D grid, batching only the GLOBAL=false AIRs to keep things simple, using a uniform block size of 128 threads where unused threads just contribute zero, and making sure the temporary buffer is zeroed before the kernel runs.\n\nI also need to account for blocks that exceed an AIR's coset count—they'll return early and leave zeros, which the reduction step handles naturally. I can reuse the existing reduction kernel from sumcheck.cuh for the per-segment work, and I'll keep zerocheck and logup batches separate since they have different kernel logic and output types.\n\nThe block-to-AIR mapping will be precomputed on the Rust side with an array tracking which AIR each block belongs to and segment offsets for each AIR.\n\nBefore I finalize the plan, I should check whether the zerocheck and logup evaluations for the same AIR depend on each other. Looking at the current code...\n\nThey're called sequentially but there's no actual data dependency—both read the same inputs but produce independent outputs. Batching would run all zerocheck evaluations first, then all logup evaluations, which changes the order but remains correct. The trade-off is memory: the current per-AIR approach keeps peak usage low by allocating and freeing sequentially, while batching allocates all metadata and output buffers at once, using more memory overall. Let me estimate the additional peak memory needed for batching across all the temporary buffers and metadata.\n\nLooking at the per-AIR device buffers for logup data—rules and weights—these are currently stack-allocated and freed when the function returns, but the kernel uses them asynchronously. With deferred synchronization, those buffers need to stay alive until the stream syncs, which means holding roughly 1-2 MB additional peak memory across all 396 AIRs. That's negligible on an 80+ GB GPU, so batching is feasible from a memory perspective.\n\nLet me trace through the actual code to understand how the buffers are currently managed...\n\nExamining the GPU kernel launch...\n\nThe issue is clear: when the function returns, all those local device buffers get dropped, but if the kernel is still running asynchronously, we're accessing freed memory. The VPMM might handle this automatically, but I need to verify how DeviceBuffer's drop implementation works and whether it properly synchronizes or manages deferred operations.\n\nLooking at the project structure, the VPMM in `openvm-cuda-common` likely uses stream-ordered allocation semantics—either through CUDA's native `cudaMallocAsync`/`cudaFreeAsync` or a custom pool that respects stream ordering. This means when a buffer is dropped, the memory stays valid until all pending operations on that stream complete, so the current deferred sync approach is actually safe. For the batching strategy, the same principle applies: I can drop per-AIR logup buffers immediately after launching the batched kernel, and the VPMM will keep them alive until execution finishes.\n\nThe key insight is the ordering: I need to keep per-AIR buffers alive through the kernel launch itself, since the metadata contains raw pointers to them. The solution is to collect all per-AIR buffers in a vector that persists until after the launch, then let them drop safely once the kernel is queued. The VPMM's stream ordering guarantees the memory won't be reused until the kernel completes.\n\nBuilding a vector to hold buffers alive... I'm thinking through a couple of edge cases: if there are no coset-parallel-eligible AIRs with GLOBAL=false, the batch stays empty and we skip the launch entirely. And if there's only one AIR to batch, it's functionally equivalent to an individual launch—no performance gain, but the code handles it fine with a batch size of 1.\n\nNow I'm ready to write out the full plan.\n\n---\n\n# Plan: Batch Round 0 Coset-Parallel Kernel Launches\n\n## Goal\n\nReduce Round 0 GPU time at APC 300 by batching ~935 sequential coset-parallel kernel launches into 2 batched launches (one for zerocheck, one for logup). Currently, each small AIR launches its own kernel with 3-12 thread blocks on a GPU with 108+ SMs, resulting in 3-11% SM utilization per launch. Batching executes all small AIRs simultaneously, targeting ~100% SM utilization.\n\nPrimary target: Round 0 APC 300 wall time < 400ms (currently 570ms).\n\n## Current Code Path\n\n1. `sumcheck_uni_round0_polys` (mod.rs:599-928) orchestrates Round 0:\n   - Phase 1 (lines 744-841): Iterates over all AIR instances. For each:\n     a. Calls `evaluate_round0_constraints_gpu` (round0.rs:31-124) → FFI → `_zerocheck_ntt_eval_constraints` (zerocheck_round0.cu:667-717)\n     b. Calls `evaluate_round0_interactions_gpu` (round0.rs:132-275) → FFI → `_logup_bary_eval_interactions_round0`\n     c. Stores results in `Round0Pending` vec\n   - Phase 2 (lines 844-924): `current_stream_sync()`, then D2H transfers and polynomial construction\n\n2. Each CUDA dispatcher checks `use_coset_parallel_mode(num_x, skip_domain)` (threshold: `num_x * skip_domain < 32768`):\n   - Small AIRs → coset-parallel kernel: grid = `(x_blocks, num_cosets)`, each block handles one coset\n   - Large AIRs → lockstep kernel: grid = `(x_blocks, 1)`, each thread handles all cosets\n\n3. Each kernel launch consists of: evaluation kernel + `final_reduce_block_sums` reduction kernel (2 GPU kernel launches per AIR per eval type).\n\n4. At APC 300, I'm looking at the performance characteristics where small AIRs dominate the launch count and underutilize the GPU.\n\nDefining metadata structs... I'm defining a `LogupBatchMeta` struct that holds metadata for the logup round, including selector and preprocessed data, constraint rules, weights computed per AIR, and various configuration parameters like buffer size and trace height.\n\nNow I'm designing a CUDA kernel for batched constraint evaluation across multiple AIRs and cosets, where the grid dimensions scale with the total number of x-blocks and cosets, using thread blocks of 128 threads and local intermediates only. I'm finishing the output buffer indexing for the zerocheck kernel, then moving to the logup interaction kernel which uses a similar batched structure but outputs `FracExt` values and applies per-AIR weights and denominators from the metadata. Now I need to write the launcher functions that will invoke these batched kernels. Now I'm setting up the kernel launch configuration with 128 threads per block and a grid spanning all x-blocks and cosets, then dispatching based on whether shared memory is needed for the domain computation. After that, I'll launch the batched evaluation kernel followed by the reduction kernel to aggregate block sums across the AIR instances. For the logup variant, the structure mirrors this but adapts for logup-specific metadata and uses double the shared memory to handle fractional extensions.\n\nNext I need to add the FFI declarations in Rust to expose these CUDA kernels, then define the corresponding metadata structs that match the C layout for safe interop between Rust and the compiled kernels.\n\nThen in the orchestration layer, I'm modifying the Phase 1 logic to classify each AIR instance based on whether it's eligible for batching—checking if the coset parallelism and buffer size fall within the thresholds—and separating them into batched and non-batched groups.\n\nFor the batch-eligible AIRs, I'm computing the block counts, building metadata structs with device pointers, and for logup cases, constructing per-AIR DAGs with weights that get uploaded to the device. Finally, I'm aggregating the total block count and determining the maximum coset dimension needed across all batched AIRs.\n\nNow I'm setting up the segment offsets as a cumulative sum to track where each AIR's blocks start, and creating a mapping from block indices back to their source AIR. For the actual batched execution, if zerocheck is involved, I upload the batch metadata and mappings, allocate temporary and output buffers on the device, then invoke the batched zerocheck kernel. Similarly for logup, I upload the corresponding batch metadata and index mappings to prepare for the batched logup computation.\n\nFor AIRs that can't be batched—either because they're not coset-parallel or have global constraints—I fall back to the existing individual GPU kernels. Once the batched kernels finish and I sync the stream, I transfer the output buffers back to host memory and slice them by AIR index to extract each one's results.\n\nFor zerocheck, the buffer size is already precomputed during key generation, so I can check eligibility upfront. For logup, the buffer size only becomes known after compiling the DAG, so I classify AIRs for batching right after that compilation step—if the buffer is small enough and the AIR is coset-parallel, it goes into the batch; otherwise it gets an individual launch. The Rust loop then processes all AIRs with this classification in place.\n\nComputing thread-to-work mapping in the batched kernel...\n\nThreads iterate over x_int values starting from their base position with a computed stride, and threads beyond the valid x range contribute nothing to the sum. I'm realizing I need to add `num_x_blocks` to the metadata to calculate the stride, but I can derive it on-the-fly from `num_x` and `skip_domain` using ceiling division instead of storing it, which adds minimal overhead per block.\n\nNow I'm looking at how the stride calculation changes between the current coset-parallel kernel and the batched version. In the current kernel, `gridDim.x` represents this AIR's blocks, so the stride naturally reflects that. For the batched kernel where each block handles one AIR, I need to compute the stride based on that specific AIR's block count rather than the global grid dimensions.\n\nI'll compute `air_x_blocks` directly in the kernel using a simple ceiling division—it's just one integer operation per block, so the overhead is negligible. Now I need to verify that the `segment_offsets` array will work correctly with the batched reduction kernel, since it expects the starting block index for each segment. [0, 3), blocks for AIR 1 have blockIdx.x in [3, 5), and so on. When the reduction kernel reads from `block_sums`, it uses the segment offsets to map each AIR's blocks correctly—for segment 0 starting at offset 0, it reads indices 0-2, and for segment 1 starting at offset 3, it reads indices 3-4. This matches exactly what the batched kernel writes out. The `segment_offsets` I'm computing on the Rust side as a cumulative sum of per-AIR x_blocks is precisely what the reduction needs, so the logic is sound. Now I'll implement this.\n\nActually, I should double-check whether zero-initializing `tmp_sums_buffer` is necessary. The batched kernel only writes to slots for blocks and cosets that exist—blocks beyond `num_cosets` return early without writing. That means for coset indices beyond an AIR's actual count, those buffer slots remain uninitialized. The reduction kernel then reads these uninitialized values and writes them to the output, which would be incorrect.\n\nBut the Rust side only reads the valid portion of the output—the slots corresponding to actual cosets. So even though we're technically reading undefined memory in CUDA, the garbage values end up in positions we ignore. Each output index is computed independently, so bad data in one slot doesn't corrupt others. It's undefined behavior, but practically harmless since we discard those results anyway.\n\nStill, zero-initializing is cheap—`cudaMemset` on these small buffers is fast enough that it's worth doing to avoid any potential issues.\n\nNow I'm writing out the plan as a markdown file. I should include `num_x_blocks` in the metadata to avoid computing it inside the kernel. It's just one uint32 and makes the kernel cleaner. I'll add it back to the struct definition.\n\nDefining metadata structure... Calculating struct memory layouts...\n\nSo ZerocheckBatchMeta comes to 96 bytes with the pointers, size_t fields, and uint32 values naturally aligned without padding. Now I'm working through LogupBatchMeta's layout with 8 pointers, a size_t, the FpExt field for denom_sum_init, and 4 more uint32 fields to see where that lands.\n\nI'm realizing FpExt's alignment depends on how it's defined—if it's 4 uint32_t values packed together, it should have 4-byte alignment, which means no padding issues. Since both the Rust and CUDA sides use `#[repr(C)]`, they'll follow the same C alignment rules and stay in sync.\n\nNow I'm writing out the actual plan document. But I just noticed something—I need to check if `lambda_combinations` and `logup_combinations` impact the round0 kernel launches. Looking at the code, there's a loop that precomputes lambda combinations for each AIR that has zerocheck monomials, calling a function to compute them based on the public key and lambda powers.\n\nThese get stored but I don't see them actually used in round0—they're probably for later rounds. The round0 evaluation uses `d_lambda_pows` directly instead. So these precomputations don't affect our batching strategy, which is good.\n\nI'm also looking at how `d_main_parts` is constructed per AIR. Building a vector of pointers to the committed trace buffers and the common main buffer,