r `num_cosets_zc == 0` or constraints are empty for zerocheck, and whether interactions exist for logup, mirroring what the current per-AIR wrappers already do.\n\nThe execution strategy is to compute all per-AIR metadata first in a shared loop without launching kernels, then issue the zerocheck batch, followed by the logup batch, and finally handle any remaining individual launches.\n\nNow I'm reconsidering the zero-initialization of the temporary sums buffer. Since blocks with `blockIdx.y >= meta.num_cosets` return early, and each AIR's blocks occupy a separate segment in the reduction, all blocks within a segment belong to the same AIR and share the same `num_cosets` value—so if `blockIdx.y` is below that threshold, every block in the segment will participate in the reduction.\n\nThe key insight is that we never actually read the output positions beyond `actual_num_cosets * skip_domain` per AIR, so even though uninitialized memory gets summed when blocks return early, those garbage values are discarded and never used. While CUDA doesn't treat uninitialized device memory as undefined behavior the way C++ does, zero-initializing is still worth doing for robustness and costs negligible time, so I'll include it in the plan.\n\nNow I'm thinking through the metadata representation for `d_main_parts` — it's a device buffer containing device pointers, so storing its address gives us the double indirection we need to match the kernel's parameter type.\n\nI've settled on the batching strategy: group only coset-parallel AIRs with small buffers, keep zerocheck and logup separate, use a 2D grid indexed by an air_for_block mapping, and store per-AIR metadata with all the pointers and scalars needed. I'm about to write this up as a design document, roughly 200-300 lines.\n\nWriting design documentation...","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10052,"cache_read_input_tokens":58719,"ca