lan handle ALL the data dependencies correctly (what needs to stay alive, what can be dropped)?\n5. Are there any hidden interactions between the zerocheck and logup batches (ordering, shared state)?\n6. Is the GLOBAL=false restriction well-justified? How many AIRs might be excluded?\n7. Is the Rust-side classification logic correct (when to batch vs not)?\n\nReturn your response in exactly this format:\n\n## Findings\n(ordered by severity, with file references)\n\n## Improvements I would consider\n(real alternatives that could better improve proof time)\n\n## Assessment\n(state whether there are remaining design-level blockers, whether the plan is prototype-ready, and what the strongest part is)","uuid":"6cdccae5-7aa5-4b36-9653-5c8b63bb5fa2","session_id":"db10821c-17ee-4d4a-acd4-fd3a20fcece6"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are reviewing an implementation plan for a CUDA GPU optimization. Your job is to find correctness holes, missing invariants, interface mismatches, places where estimated wins aren't supported by measurements, and places where the plan is overcomplicated.\n\nRead these files in order:\n1. `/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt/review_guideline.md` — the review standard to follow\n2. `/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt/context.md` — project context\n3. `/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/task.md` — the task being planned\n4. `/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/2026-04-10-2049-batch-round0-coset-parallel/plan.md` — the plan to review\n\nThen verify the plan against the actual source code. Key files to check:\n- `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs` (lines 599-928) — Rust orchestration\n- `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/round0.rs` — per-AIR wrappers\n- `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/zerocheck_round0.cu` — CUDA zerocheck kernels and launchers\n- `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/logup_round0.cu` — CUDA logup kernels and launchers\n- `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/include/sumcheck.cuh` (lines 258-291) — batched_final_reduce_block_sums\n- `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/eval_config.cuh` — launch config\n- `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/include/dag_entry.cuh` (lines 16-28) — NttEvalContext\n- `crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/logup_zerocheck.rs` (lines 280-360, 920-1020) — FFI declarations and wrappers\n\nVerify specifically:\n1. Does the proposed metadata struct layout match what the existing kernel code actually needs?\n2. Is the thread-to-work mapping in the batched kernel correct (tidx, ntt_idx, x_int_base, x_int_stride)?\n3. Is the tmp_sums_buffer layout compatible with batched_final_reduce_block_sums?\n4. Does the plan handle ALL the data dependencies correctly (what needs to stay alive, what can be dropped)?\n5. Are there any hidden interactions between the zerocheck and logup batches (ordering, shared state)?\n6. Is the GLOBAL=false restriction well-justified? How many AIRs might be excluded?\n7. Is the Rust-side classification logic correct (when to batch vs not)?\n\nReturn your response in exactly this format:\n\n## Findings\n(ordered by severity, with file references)\n\n## Improvements I would consider\n(real alternatives that could better improve proof time)\n\n## Assessment\n(state whether there are remaining design-level blockers, whether the plan is prototype-ready, and what the strongest part is)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01RTQaGf1Hmu6PEd1Wge8mzZ","session_id":"db10821c-17ee-4d4a-acd4-fd3a20fcece6","uuid":"d797e59b-5053-4ac0-927c-054979cc0791","timestamp":"2026-04-10T19:11:27.929Z"}
