{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RFZczjb3DfhGS5S4UH6guV","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Task: Batch Round 0 Coset-Parallel Kernel Launches\n2\t\n3\tTask name: 2026-04-10-2049-batch-round0-coset-parallel\n4\t\n5\t## Problem Statement\n6\t\n7\tRound 0 of constraint evaluation is the worst-scaling component in STARK excl trace: 570ms at APC 300 vs 174ms at APC 0 (3.28x increase), despite total constraint instances *decreasing* by 2.23x. This inverse scaling accounts for +396ms of overhead and is the single largest contributor preventing STARK excl trace from scaling with workload reduction.\n8\t\n9\t**Root cause: GPU SM underutilization.** Nsight profiling shows Round 0 at APC 300 launches ~935 `coset_parallel` kernels (539 zerocheck + 396 logup), each processing a single small AIR instance. These kernels have tiny grids (3-12 thread blocks) on a GPU with 108+ SMs, resulting in 3-11% SM utilization per kernel. The kernels execute sequentially on a single CUDA stream, so the GPU processes them one after another, wasting 89-97% of its compute capacity on each launch.\n10\t\n11\tProfiling breakdown at APC 300:\n12\t- `zerocheck_ntt_evaluate_constraints_coset_parallel_kernel<true,false>`: 220ms, 539 instances (avg 408us, median 275us)\n13\t- `logup_r0_ntt_eval_interactions_coset_parallel_kernel<true,false>`: 195ms, 396 instances (avg 493us, median 378us)\n14\t- `*_coset_parallel_kernel<false,false>` variants: 49ms combined, 535 instances\n15\t- Total coset_parallel GPU time: **~464ms** (81% of Round 0's 570ms wall clock)\n16\t- Non-coset-parallel kernels (larger AIRs): ~96ms\n17\t- Phase 2 CPU processing: ~10ms\n18\t\n19\tAt APC 0, coset_parallel kernels total only ~60ms (137+small instances) because there are only 99 AIR instances with larger traces that better utilize the GPU.\n20\t\n21\tKey code locations:\n22\t- Round 0 orchestration: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs:599-928` (`sumcheck_uni_round0_polys`, Phase 1 loop at lines 744-841)\n23\t- Per-AIR zerocheck wrapper: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/round0.rs:31-124` (`evaluate_round0_constraints_gpu`)\n24\t- Per-AIR logup wrapper: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/round0.rs:132-275` (`evaluate_round0_interactions_gpu`)\n25\t- CUDA zerocheck coset_parallel kernel: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/zerocheck_round0.cu:335-467`\n26\t- CUDA logup coset_parallel kernel: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/logup_round0.cu:323-460`\n27\t- Launcher dispatch (threshold check): `zerocheck_round0.cu:667-717`, `logup_round0.cu:658-810`\n28\t- `COSET_PARALLEL_THRESHOLD = 32768` in both files\n29\t- Existing batched reduction: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/include/sumcheck.cuh:261-291` (`batched_final_reduce_block_sums`)\n30\t\n31\t## Proposed Approach\n32\t\n33\tBatch multiple small AIR instances into single kernel launches using a per-AIR metadata approach. This follows the same pattern that successfully optimized Stacked Reduction (task 2026-04-10-2145), which achieved 2.1x improvement by batching ~10,348 degenerate window launches into single kernel launches.\n34\t\n35\t**Mechanism:** For each coset_parallel kernel (zerocheck and logup):\n36\t\n37\t1. **Classify** AIR instances into coset_parallel-eligible (small, `num_x * skip_domain < 32768`) vs non-coset-parallel (large), using the existing threshold check already in the launchers.\n38\t\n39\t2. **Create per-AIR metadata struct** containing: pointers to trace data (`main_parts`), constraint bytecodes (`d_rules`, `d_used_nodes`), selectors, eq values, public values, lambda powers, interaction weights; plus scalar parameters (`buffer_size`, `skip_domain`, `num_x`, `height`, `num_cosets`, `g_shift`). Upload metadata array to GPU via single H2D copy.\n40\t\n41\t3. **Launch batched kernel** with grid dimensions `(max_x_blocks, max_num_cosets, num_airs_in_batch)`. Each AIR's thread blocks are identified by `blockIdx.z`. Each block loads its AIR's metadata, bounds-checks (early return if `blockIdx.x >= this_air_x_blocks` or `blockIdx.y >= this_air_num_cosets`), then evaluates constraints exactly as the current single-AIR kernel does.\n42\t\n43\t4. **Batched reduction**: Use the existing `batched_final_reduce_block_sums` in `sumcheck.cuh:261-291`, which already supports segment offsets for multi-AIR reduction. Each AIR's partial sums are written to a contiguous region of a shared output buffer, with per-AIR offsets tracked in the segment array.\n44\t\n45\t5. **Non-coset-parallel AIRs** continue using the existing per-AIR kernel path (these are already well-utilizing the GPU with large grids).\n46\t\n47\t6. **Rust-side orchestration** in `sumcheck_uni_round0_polys` Phase 1: split the per-AIR iteration into two sub-phases — (a) collect metadata for small AIRs and issue a single batched launch, (b) issue individual launches for large AIRs. Phase 2 processing remains unchanged.\n48\t\n49\tThe batched kernel code would be structurally identical to the existing coset_parallel kernels, with the addition of `blockIdx.z` indexing into the metadata array and per-AIR bounds checking. Shared memory layout is unchanged (each block uses its own shared memory independently).\n50\t\n51\t## Expected Outcome\n52\t\n53\t- **Round 0 APC 300**: Reduce from 570ms to ~250-350ms (150-300ms savings). The coset_parallel portion should improve 2-4x from better SM utilization — multiple small AIRs process concurrently across different SMs instead of sequentially.\n54\t- **STARK excl trace APC 300**: Reduce from 2205ms to ~1905-2055ms (7-14% improvement vs current, 15-22% vs baseline 2478ms).\n55\t- **Scaling ratio** (APC 300 / APC 0 STARK excl trace): Improve from 1.027 toward ~0.90 or lower.\n56\t- **APC 0**: Negligible change (only 99 instances, most already use non-coset-parallel path with good GPU utilization).\n57\t\n58\t## Success Criteria\n59\t\n60\t1. **Primary**: Round 0 APC 300 wall time < 400ms (currently 570ms, target >30% reduction).\n61\t2. **Secondary**: STARK excl trace APC 300 < 2050ms (currently 2205ms, target >7% reduction).\n62\t3. **Scaling**: STARK excl trace APC 300 / APC 0 ratio < 0.97 (currently 1.027).\n63\t4. **Correctness**: All 94 `openvm-cuda-backend` tests pass. Proof verification succeeds for APC 0/100/300.\n64\t\n65\t## Considered Alternatives\n66\t\n67\t1. **Rayon-parallelize Round 0 Phase 2 CPU processing** (estimated ~10ms savings at APC 300). Phase 2 is only ~10ms wall time because the Phase 1 deferred-sync optimization (task 2026-04-10-1933) moved the bottleneck to GPU execution. Far too small to justify as a standalone task.\n68\t\n69\t2. **Multi-stream Round 0 (launch per-AIR kernels on N CUDA streams)** (estimated 100-200ms savings). Achieves better GPU utilization without new kernel code by letting the GPU scheduler interleave blocks from different streams. However, requires refactoring the single-stream architecture in `cuda-common`, per-stream temporary buffer allocation (higher memory), and still has per-kernel CPU launch overhead (~28ms for 935 launches). Batched kernel approach is more efficient, has lower memory overhead, and follows the proven pattern from stacked reduction.\n70\t\n71\t3. **Batch LogUp GKR input evaluation kernels** (estimated 100-250ms savings). The `evaluate_interactions_gkr_kernel` totals 427ms at APC 300 with 791 instances and has similar SM underutilization. However, the GKR protocol has sequential layer dependencies that limit overall pipeline improvement, and the kernel's interaction data structures are more complex to batch. Round 0 coset_parallel has a larger absolute improvement potential (464ms target vs 427ms) and is simpler to batch since the existing `batched_final_reduce_block_sums` can be reused directly. GKR batching is a good follow-up target.\n72\t\n73\t4. **Batch non-degenerate stacked reduction kernels** (estimated 70-86ms savings). The non-degenerate path still has 7467 per-window kernel launches contributing ~86ms of CPU overhead at APC 300. Moderate impact but smaller than Round 0 batching. Good follow-up target after this optimization.\n74\t\n75\t5. **Cache `calculate_zero_hash` results by trace height** (initially estimated ~100ms savings). Investigation revealed this kernel is in the openvm VM memory Merkle tree (`openvm/crates/vm/cuda/src/system/memory/merkle_tree.cu`), part of the Set Initial Memory phase — NOT within STARK excl trace. Out of scope.\n76\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01NuzYvE5zkA1q9Vs1qoXDoc","session_id":"db10821c-17ee-4d4a-acd4-fd3a20fcece6","uuid":"487551f7-55f5-493f-ad8a-094dba46369f","timestamp":"2026-04-10T19:15:55.014Z"}
